      "text": "To solve for $x$ in the equation $x^2 = 729$, we start by taking the square root of both sides of the equation. This gives us:\n\n$$\\sqrt{x^2} = \\sqrt{729}.$$\n\nThe square root of $x^2$ is $x$ by the property of square roots, and the square root of 729 is $\\sqrt{3^6}$. Since the square root of a number squared returns the absolute value of the number, we have:\n\n$$|x| = \\left|\\sqrt{3^6}\\right|.$$\n\nSimplifying the left side gives us $\\boxed{27}$, and the right side becomes $\\boxed{27}$ when simplified:\n\n$$x = \\pm 27.$$\n\nHowever, as per the initial condition, $x$ is positive, so there is no need to consider negative values. Therefore, $x = \\boxed{27}$.",
